### The stack: the open PR's commits and fixups

The design's **Stack** section, with deltas applied, is the ordered list of
PRs this workflow ships, one commit and one branch each. An entry is
**assembled** when it is in the stack you were given; the **open PR** is the
first entry that is not. The log says what earlier rounds did and why, and
is what the `pr-done` and `done` walks read; it never decides which entry
is open. Every commit you make is either
**the open PR's** or **a fixup to one assembled PR**, never both.

- A change belongs to an assembled PR when a reviewer of that PR, reading it
  after the stack is final, would see it as part of what that PR does: a
  defect in code it introduced, a reviewer finding against it, a delta
  re-scoping it. A change belongs to the open PR when the effective stack
  assigns the work to the open PR, even where it edits lines an earlier PR
  added — extending in PR 3 a function PR 1 introduced is PR 3's work.
- A fixup is `git commit --fixup=refs/heads/<branch>`: git writes the
  subject `fixup! <that PR's title>`, which is how the assembler finds its
  target. It holds only that PR's changes — one fixup per target PR per
  increment or respond round — and its log entry names the target branch
  and the finding or delta item it lands.
- A fixup is an ordinary commit in every other way: hooks pass, nothing is
  amended or rebased, and no other branch is ever checked out. Folding it
  into its PR is the pr-assembler's restack, the one history rewrite in the
  chain.
- A fixup never touches the **changelog** — the pr-assembler owns it;
  implementers add no entries — and never the **log**, which in a stack
  workflow lives outside the repository.
{% if followup %}

In a re-created stack (the launch recorded a base; the design's **Stack**
says so), an open entry whose branch already exists is an inherited one; a
new entry's branch never does, and a new entry is built as any open entry
is. An inherited entry's round starts by replaying the shipped PR onto the
PR base: `git cherry-pick --no-commit refs/heads/<branch>`, the branch's
one commit, conflicts resolved as the entry's `scope` says the PR changes
at the new base, then `git commit -C refs/heads/<branch>`, an ordinary
commit that passes the hooks (a bare cherry-pick that applies cleanly runs
none). The cycle's changes to that PR follow as the open PR's own commits, never as fixups, since the
entry is not assembled. The replay is one increment whatever its size; the
increment target and stop apply to the work written in this cycle. Nothing
is rebased, amended or checked out: the inherited branch stays where it is
until the assembler moves it.
{% endif %}
{% endif %}

