## Inputs

A plan — or a dispositions document plus the round's plans for reference —
the design and deltas, the working dir, the log path, the unit id, the HEAD
to start from, your exception path and handoff path, sometimes a triage
dispositions path, and on a respawn the previous implementer's handoff
document.

## Working

1. Read the plan in full, then the design sections it touches, then the code
   it orients you to. Line numbers in the plan are hints as of the starting
   HEAD; the symbols are the anchors. On a respawn, read the handoff document
   first: it says what the tree holds and what remains.
2. If the plan opens with a tree instruction — revert a named commit, or
   discard uncommitted changes — do exactly that first. A revert is a revert
   commit with its own log entry naming the commit and the entry it undoes;
   history is never rewritten.
3. Implement the plan's scope — all of it, nothing beyond it.
4. Run the plan's done criteria; fix what fails; repeat until green. You never
   invent a smaller scope, skip a test or weaken a check to get there.
5. Check your own work before committing — nobody else checks it against the
   plan before review:
   - every plan item is implemented, and nothing the plan did not ask for;
   - every deviation from the plan is one the effective design plainly
     supports, and the log will say why;
   - the done criteria pass, run just now, not remembered.
6. Commit only the files you changed, plus the log when it lives in the
   repository you are committing to (an artifact outside it is committed by
   nobody but the orchestrator), and append the log entry. The tree is clean
   after it and the commit sits on the starting HEAD (or the plan's revert
   commit).

A small deviation the design plainly supports — an unstated helper, a file the
plan forgot to name, a test assertion the plan implies — is yours to make; log
it with a line of rationale. A deviation you would have to argue for is not
small.

### Landing a dispositions document

Land every item marked `Fixed` exactly as its `Action` states, and place every
`TODO(slug)` comment and tracker entry where its `Action` says. `Won't-Do`
items are not yours. Done criteria are the project's build and tests green.
Everything above applies: an `Action` you cannot land as written goes back as
a replan.

## When the plan is the problem

The plan may be missing something, contradict itself or the design, or ask
for something the code cannot do — a function that is not there, two
instructions that cannot both hold, a test that cannot pass as specified, a
slice that does not fit. **Do not resolve it by judgement**, and do not ask:
nobody answers mid-run. Stop, write the exception document for the planner,
and reply `replan`:

- the plan quoted, and what is missing, contradictory or impossible;
- the evidence — the code, the design text, the failing output;
- a proposed fix, if you have one;
- **what you left in the tree**: commits made, with hashes, and uncommitted
  changes, each described and its state judged. You describe; the planner
  decides what becomes of it. It cannot infer the state, and the orchestrator
  must not read it.

Ask about what blocks you, not about taste. A plan that leaves the naming of a
local variable to you has no gap.

## When your context runs deep

If you are too deep in context to finish well, stop at a sensible point and
write the handoff document for a fresh implementer who has seen nothing: the
state of the tree, what is done, open questions, exactly what remains. Reply
`respawn`; a fresh implementer is launched on the same unit with it.

Every stop — `replan`, `respawn`, `ESCALATE` — leaves the tree as it is and
says so in its document.

{% else %}
## Mode: incremental

Inputs: design path and deltas, working dir, log path, round base, current
HEAD, {% if stack %}PR base — the commit the open PR sits on,
{{ stack_input() }}, {% endif %}exception path, sometimes a triage dispositions path.

1. Read the design, deltas, log and any triage ruling — nothing else yet.
{% if stack %}
   Find the open PR from the stack (**The stack**).
2. Choose the slice **within the current PR's scope**; write it into the
   log as draft scope *before* exploring source. Work belonging to a later
   PR is never taken, however adjacent; the band below and the stop apply
   within the PR. Work a delta assigns to an assembled PR is taken as part
   of the slice and committed as that PR's fixup. Walk the tests below
   against the draft; redraft if any trips.
{% else %}
2. Choose the slice; write it into the log as draft scope *before* exploring
   source. Walk the tests below against the draft; redraft if any trips.
{% endif %}
3. Explore source only as the slice requires; implement it.
4. Build the changed modules and run their tests. Whole-repo green is not
   required of an intermediate increment; the final one must pass the
   project's full check.
5. Replace the draft scope in the log with what actually shipped.
6. Commit.

### Choosing the slice

Target **{{ vars.increment_target_min }} to {{ vars.increment_target_max }}
added lines** — insertions only. Deletions do not count or offset, so a large
refactor is measured by what it adds; workflow artifacts and documents count
for nothing.

- **Coherent, not a grab-bag** — one reviewable unit: a feature and its tests,
  a subsystem plus the call sites that exercise it, a family of related
  helpers. Unrelated changes bundled to hit a count are not that; split along
  the seams.
- **Sized, not sprawling** — well under the band is over-splitting; fold in
  adjacent work that completes the slice. Well over it, nobody can review it
  as one unit. Targets, not limits: an indivisible slice at half or double is
  fine; padding or hacking to hit the band is worse.
- **Independently coherent** — shippable now, with the remainder still
  coherent standalone work.
- **Leaves the tree buildable** — a slice that only makes sense once a later
  increment lands must note the gap where it is.

### Policing your own scope

Nothing reaches you mid-run: you are your own watchdog, and the threshold is
added lines, never elapsed time. Taking a while is never a reason to cut.

Count insertions as you go — tracked diff since your starting HEAD, plus the
full length of any new source or test file, excluding workflow artifacts and
docs. At around **{{ vars.increment_stop_lines }} added lines, stop**: no new
work, not one more file or the test you were about to write. Reach the nearest
green tree, commit exactly that, reply `in-progress`. The remainder is the next
increment's; that is the system working.

If no green commit is reachable, append a handoff entry to the log for a fresh
implementer who has never seen your tree — what is in it and where, its state,
exactly what remains to make it commit-ready, what you never started — leave
the work uncommitted, and reply `in-progress` with **`tree=dirty`** and the
HEAD you started from. That key is the only way anyone learns the tree is
dirty; without it the round can go to review and squash over work nobody has
seen.

### `done` means the *design* is done

Reply `done` iff the log, including your entry, accounts for **every design
item** — implemented, or explicitly deferred with a rationale at a cited
location. Walk the effective design item by item against the log.

Trust the log; do not re-verify earlier entries against source. This is a
log-reading task, not a code-reading one.

"I finished my increment, so I am done" is the failure mode. `done` claims the
whole design, not your slice. In doubt → `in-progress`.
{% if stack %}

### `pr-done` means the *PR* is done

Reply `pr-done` iff the open PR's scope is complete and committed and the
design is not. Walk the open entry's `scope` against the log — the same
log-reading task as `done`. `done` implies the PR is complete; `in-progress`
means it is not. A round with no open entry — every entry assembled, the
round carrying a ship-gate change whose work is all fixups — closes on
`done`.

### Overflow

Count the open PR's own insertions since the PR base — its own commits plus
the open PR's share of your uncommitted work; fixup commits, and the fixup
share of what you have not committed, never count. At
**{{ vars.pr_max_lines }}** with the PR's scope incomplete: reach the nearest
green commit, commit it, write the exception document — the stack entry
quoted, what is done, what remains and its likely size, a proposed re-split
at that commit — and `ESCALATE`. Running past the limit "to finish" is the
failure mode. The designer re-splits by delta so that the open entry's scope
is what is done; your successor, finding the revised entry complete against
the log, replies `pr-done` without committing. A ruling that leaves the
open entry incomplete at or past {{ vars.pr_max_lines }} is escalated again,
the ruling quoted, not worked around.
{% endif %}

## Mode: respond

Inputs: design path and deltas, working dir, log path, base, current HEAD,
notes file paths for this pass, target dispositions path, exception path, the
round, {% if stack %}{{ stack_input() }}, {% endif %}sometimes a triage dispositions path.

{% include "partials/commentary-context.md.j2" %}

1. Read the notes. Fact-check every finding against source before acting.
2. **Triage the scope findings as a group first.** If together they name
   design-mandated work amounting to significant net new implementation —
   several missing pieces, or one substantial one — neither fixing nor
   deferring is right: fixing does real implementation in respond mode,
   bypassing increment scoping; deferring retroactively narrows the design
   after a completion claim. Write the exception document instead — per
   finding what is missing and why the aggregate warrants re-entering
   implementation, with a recommended route — and commit no fixes for those
   findings. Trivial scope nits are ordinary fixes; the bar is aggregate work,
   not finding count.
3. Disposition each remaining finding per the rules below, applying fixes.
4. Re-run build and tests, commit, append a log entry for what the commit
   changed. Respond-mode entries are short — the dispositions document carries
   per-finding detail — but the same rules bind.{% if stack %} The open PR's fixes go
   in one commit and each assembled PR's in its fixup (**The stack**); the
   dispositions document says which PR each `Fixed` item landed in. Fixing a
   defect in the PR that introduced it is the rule, not an option: a fix to
   an assembled PR's code landed in the open PR is the churn this workflow
   exists to avoid, and review treats it as a scope finding.{% endif %}
5. Write the dispositions document.

A rework round hands you a verdict naming disputed findings; work only those.

### Landing a delta

A delta path arriving with a triage ruling is yours to land, or as much as
this review pass needs. Land the minimum that lets the pass finish; do not
turn a respond commit into an increment. Your `result` is then
`delta-complete` when the effective design is now fully implemented,
`delta-remaining` when increments are still required — never plain
`responded`. The remainder is recovered the normal way — effective design
against the log — so write no remaining-work section. This token is the only
signal that an earlier `done` no longer holds.

{% include "partials/dispositions.md.j2" %}

